# Environment configuration. Copy it to `.env` and edit it as needed:
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is gitignored and holds a token -- do not commit it.
#
# ★★★ There are **no built-in defaults** here: every entry below must be set in `.env`,
#   a missing one makes the script fail and name the key.
#
#   A default is a fallback —— it makes people believe "the script connected to the right machine / found the right directory",
#   when it actually guessed. **Path-finding heuristics are especially forbidden** ("walk up from the current directory",
#   "try a few common locations", "scan for something that looks right"); measured case: `_guess_workspace()`
#   walked up six levels from the CWD and then guessed a home directory; run it from another directory and it may connect to a different workspace,
#   while these scripts write files there and tests create and delete documents in it.
#
# Precedence: real environment variable > .env. A temporary override does not require editing the file:
#
#   SIYUAN_PORT=6807 python3 scripts/verify-live.py

# ---------- SiYuan (the user instance, the one you normally work in) ----------
SIYUAN_SCHEME=http
SIYUAN_HOST=127.0.0.1
SIYUAN_PORT=6806
# The SiYuan workspace directory. Must be given explicitly —— it is used to read the token, deploy the plugin and compute plugin directories.
SIYUAN_WORKSPACE=/path/to/siyuan/workspace

# API token. Leave it empty to read it from <SIYUAN_WORKSPACE>/conf/conf.json
# (that is the authoritative location, not a guess). Set it here explicitly only when it cannot be read.
SIYUAN_TOKEN=

# The plugin's directory name inside SiYuan, equal to the name field of plugin.json
PLUGIN_NAME=siyuan-native-outline-mindmap

# ---------- Isolated test instance (E2E / verify:android may use only this one) ----------
# ★ These keys and the group above are **two different instances**. The test suite really creates and deletes documents,
#   so the code must **not** silently fall back between them —— the user explicitly required on 2026-10-10
#   "no silent switching to 6806".
#
# The port deliberately avoids SIYUAN_PORT so it does not collide with the instance you are using.
SIYUAN_TEST_PORT=6807
# The instance root directory. It holds resources/ (the downloaded official binaries), workspace/ (an independent workspace),
# kernel.pid, kernel.log.
# Start the instance with `python3 scripts/test-instance.py` (downloads the official binaries and creates an independent workspace).
TEST_INSTANCE_DIR=/path/to/repo/.tmp/test-instance

# ---------- Deploy targets ----------
# Which plugin directories `npm run deploy` syncs the build output to. **Colon-separated** (the same syntax as PATH),
# as many as you list are deployed.
#
# ★ Several entries must be possible: the main instance and the test instance each hold a plugin copy, and deploying only one makes the tests
#   run against old code —— measured case: "the fix was deployed but the regression test still reported the old behavior".
PLUGIN_DIRS=/path/to/siyuan/workspace/data/plugins/siyuan-native-outline-mindmap:/path/to/repo/.tmp/test-instance/workspace/data/plugins/siyuan-native-outline-mindmap

# ---------- SiYuan source (read-only reference) ----------
# Many contracts of this plugin were verified against the SiYuan source (protyle / kernel API / plugin mechanism),
# and debugging often requires going back to check "how SiYuan actually implements it".
#
# ★ Set it explicitly instead of running a full-disk find every time —— that is both slow and likely to miss.
#   Verified landmarks (2026-10-08):
#     · app/src/protyle            editor (outline DOM, contenteditable)
#     · kernel/model/plugin.go     plugin loading (how CSS is injected into the host page)
#     · app/src/plugin/index.ts    plugin API contract (addTab / addDock / addCommand)
#
# This one **may be left empty**: that is an **explicit statement** that "this machine does not have the source" (not a guess),
# checks that need the source skip and say why.
SIYUAN_SRC=

# ---------- Chromium CDP (needed by the live-device check / screen recording) ----------
# How to start it: chromium --headless --remote-debugging-port=<CDP_PORT> ...
CDP_HOST=127.0.0.1
CDP_PORT=9333

# ---------- Android device / container joint test (Redroid / phone + CDP) ----------
# adb device serial (the column in `adb devices`, e.g. 127.0.0.1:5555 or a physical device serial).
# Must be given explicitly: with several devices attached, the script must not pick one for you.
ANDROID_SERIAL=127.0.0.1:5555
# Browser package name (e.g. com.brave.browser or com.android.chrome)
ANDROID_BROWSER_PKG=com.brave.browser
# Host-side port forwarded to the phone for the browser CDP
ANDROID_CDP_PORT=9222

# ---------- Toolchain ----------
# The Chromium binary the E2E suite launches (`npm run test:e2e`).
#
# ★ Required, with no fallback and no "try the system one" chain: which binary is launched
#   decides the rendering engine, so the suite's conclusions only hold for the browser it names.
#   A silent switch to a different build would keep every case green while measuring something else.
# Find one with:  which chromium  |  ls /usr/bin/*chromium*
CHROMIUM_PATH=/path/to/chromium

# ---------- Toolchain ----------
# The python interpreter used to run verification scripts that "need playwright".
# Currently only `npm run verify:android` (scripts/verify-android.py) needs it,
# every other script uses the standard library only.
#
# ★ Why it is configured separately: python playwright is often installed in only one distribution or virtualenv
#   (on this machine only in miniforge), while `npm run verify:android` runs the
#   `python3` found on PATH, and npm cannot read `.env` —— so following the docs fails with a
#   ModuleNotFoundError.
#   ⚠️ Besides, running only `--scenario shot` does not go through CDP and still succeeds,
#      which looks exactly like "the toolchain is fine" -- an easy misjudgement.
#
# The path must be given explicitly (no "make do with the current interpreter" —— that makes you believe it is configured).
# Once it is set, the script re-executes itself with that interpreter; the entry point does not change.
# First find out which interpreter has playwright installed:
#
#   for p in /usr/bin/python3 ~/miniforge3/bin/python3; do
#     $p -c "import playwright" 2>/dev/null && echo "$p available"
#   done
PYTHON=/path/to/python3
